From: Elie Bursztein <github@elie.net>
Subject: [PATCH] fuse: refuse POSIX ACLs for daemons without FUSE_POSIX_ACL

A FUSE daemon that does not negotiate FUSE_POSIX_ACL gets ACL writes
forwarded as opaque system.posix_acl_* xattrs when it is mounted in the
initial user namespace. The kernel never reads them back for permission
checks (fuse_get_inode_acl returns NULL), and it leaves applying the
equivalent mode to the daemon.

Apple's Virtualization.framework virtio-fs server does neither: it stores
the xattr on the host file and leaves the mode alone. coreutils cp -a,
cp -p and mv across filesystems set the final mode only through
system.posix_acl_access, so a 0640 file copied into the share stayed at
the 0600 cp created it with, and the host file kept an inert ACL xattr.

Refuse ACL get and set for every daemon without FUSE_POSIX_ACL, as the
kernel already does outside the initial user namespace. Tools then fall
back to chmod, which every virtio-fs server applies.

---
--- a/fs/fuse/acl.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/acl.c
@@ -58,10 +58,14 @@
 {
 	/*
 	 * Refuse interacting with POSIX ACLs for daemons that
-	 * don't support FUSE_POSIX_ACL and are not mounted on
-	 * the host to retain backwards compatibility.
+	 * don't support FUSE_POSIX_ACL. Upstream keeps passing
+	 * them through as opaque xattrs for mounts in the initial
+	 * user namespace; a daemon that stores them without
+	 * applying them (Apple's virtio-fs server) then silently
+	 * drops the mode a tool like cp -a sets through the ACL.
+	 * Refusing makes such tools fall back to chmod.
 	 */
-	return !fc->posix_acl && (i_user_ns(inode) != &init_user_ns);
+	return !fc->posix_acl;
 }
 
 struct posix_acl *fuse_get_acl(struct mnt_idmap *idmap,
